<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Mariana Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> are a chain of islands in the western Pacific north of Guam. Spanish until 1899, they were sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1914 and placed under American administration as part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands in 1947. Since 1978 they have been a commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands.jpg" alt="Northern Mariana Islands" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Northern_Mariana_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Northern Mariana Islands are the 14 northern islands of the Mariana Archipelago, whose southernmost island, Guam, is a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> territory. Their area is about 475 square kilometres. The southern islands are limestone, with level terraces and fringing coral reefs, while the northern islands are volcanic, with active volcanoes on several, including Anatahan, Pagan and Agrihan. Mount Agrihan is the highest point, at 965 m. The climate is tropical rainforest, with a dry season from December to June and a rainy season, which can bring typhoons, from July to November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> were the first islands settled by humans in Remote Oceania, around 1500 to 1400 BCE, by people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Ferdinand Magellan, sailing under the Spanish flag, reached Guam in 1521 and claimed the archipelago for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which made the islands part of the Spanish East Indies in 1565 and in the 18th century forced the people of the northern Marianas to relocate.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded Guam to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and sold the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under the German–Spanish Treaty of 1899. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> they became part of the Japanese Mandate under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a> in 1944 ended Japanese rule, and afterwards the islands were administered by the United States as part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands. In 1975 nearly 80% voted to become a commonwealth of the United States, and in 1977 over 93% approved the constitution of the commonwealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands is an unincorporated territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> whose residents are United States citizens. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 47,329 people, most of them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a> and Rota, the only permanently populated islands. The administrative centre is Capitol Hill on Saipan. The northern islands were evacuated because of volcanic threat, and Pagan has been largely uninhabited since a 1981 eruption.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern Mariana Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That year Germany bought the Caroline, Palau and <b>Northern Mariana Islands</b> from Spain, and the Marshall Islands Protectorate, including Nauru, was merged in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1947 the United Nations placed the Marshall, Caroline and <b>Northern Mariana Islands</b> under American administration as the only strategic trust territory, giving the United States the right to fortify them.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li></ul></section>
